{
  // Dependency-vulnerability gate for CI (replaces a bare `npm audit --audit-level=high`,
  // which has no way to record a reasoned exception).
  //
  // The gate stays sharp: anything high/critical that is NOT listed below fails the build.
  // `allowlist` is per-advisory (GHSA id), never per-severity — we do not lower the level.
  //
  // Adding an entry requires, in this file: (1) the advisory id, (2) why it does not apply to
  // THIS codebase, measured — not assumed, (3) an expiry date, (4) what removes the need for it.
  // An expired entry is a task, not a renewal: re-check it, do not extend it by reflex.
  //
  // Prefer the fix. Everything that had an adoptable fix on 2026-07-29 was taken instead of
  // excepted (react-router-dom 7.16.0 → 7.18.1; postcss 8.5.15 → 8.5.24; vite 8.0.14 → 8.1.5;
  // and `overrides` for esbuild ^0.28.1 / js-yaml ^4.3.0 / brace-expansion@5 ^5.0.8). That
  // took the report from high 5 / moderate 2 / low 1 down to two entries.
  //
  // 2026-08-04 (#203): one of those two is gone, by fix rather than by argument. GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895
  // extended the brace-expansion range to include **5.0.8 itself** — the version the 2026-07-29
  // override had named as the remedy — so the pinned fix stopped being a fix. Patched releases
  // existed for all three locked copies (1.1.16 → 1.1.18, 2.1.3 → 2.1.4, 5.0.8 → 5.0.9), so the
  // lockfile was refreshed and the GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg entry was deleted rather than re-argued.
  // The lesson is worth keeping: an entry that names a specific fixed version carries an expiry
  // whether or not one is written next to it.
  //
  // Note the `@5` on the brace-expansion override: it is scoped on purpose, and it STAYS scoped
  // even though no allowlist entry explains it any more. `eslint-plugin-import` and
  // `eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y` still bundle `minimatch@3.1.5`, which does
  // `const expand = require('brace-expansion')` and calls it; brace-expansion@5 exports an
  // **object**, not a function, so a blanket `brace-expansion: ^5` makes every brace pattern
  // throw `expand is not a function` (it broke `npm run lint` outright on nene-invoice #732).
  // Scoping leaves those plugins on the v1 line, which now has its own patched 1.1.18.
  // Measured after the 2026-08-04 refresh, with a negative control:
  // `minimatch('src/a.ts', 'src/{a,b}.ts')` → true, `minimatch('src/c.ts', 'src/{a,b}.ts')` → false.
  // Check with: npm ls minimatch --all
  //
  // Policy: docs/development/dependency-audit.md
  "$schema": "https://github.com/IBM/audit-ci/raw/main/docs/schema.json",
  "moderate": false,
  "high": true,
  "critical": true,
  "allowlist": [
    // Removed 2026-08-15 (#212): GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2 (react-router, high) is REMOVED, not
    // renewed — and not for the reason this entry anticipated. Its stated removal condition was
    // the react-router v8 migration; what actually removed it was a change on the advisory's
    // side. On **2026-08-07T18:16:54Z the advisory itself was updated**: the single
    // 7.x-through-8.x range was split in two and a 7.x fix appeared. Re-read from the primary
    // source on 2026-08-15:
    //   gh api /advisories/GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2
    //     { react-router, ">= 7.12.0, < 7.18.2", first_patched 7.18.2 }
    //     { react-router, ">= 8.0.0,  < 8.3.0",  first_patched 8.3.0  }
    // This PR moves this tree to react-router 7.18.2, lockfile-only — no `package.json` change,
    // the existing caret already covered it. Measured in THIS tree, before and after:
    // `npm audit` high 2 -> 0; lockfile `packages` 713 -> 713 (unchanged);
    // 2 entries moved, all upgrades, no downgrades.
    //
    // The lesson, kept because the next entry inherits it: this exception was written *well* —
    // the advisory was re-read at authoring time and the "does not apply here" argument was
    // measured against this codebase. It was still invalidated days later by a change on the
    // advisory's side, and nothing in this file would have said so. **An expiry date catches an
    // entry that runs out; it does not catch an entry that stops being true.** While an id sits
    // in this list the audit gate is silent about it, so "CI is green" and "we are not exposed"
    // are not the same statement. Re-read the advisory when you touch this file, not at expiry.
    //
    // Kept below, verbatim: the measurement this entry carried. It applies again when this tree
    // moves to react-router v8 — the v8 range (>= 8.0.0, < 8.3.0) is still open. What follows
    // was true when it was written; the "no fix in the 7.x line" part is what 08-07 changed.
    // GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2 — react-router "RSC Mode CSRF Bypass Allows Action Execution Before
    // 400 Response" (high). Vulnerable range 7.12.0–8.2.0. There is NO fix in the 7.x line:
    // `react-router-dom` ends at 7.18.1 (measured `npm view react-router-dom version`), and the
    // fix lands in `react-router` >= 8.2.1 — a different package and a breaking major.
    //
    // Why it does not apply here (measured 2026-07-29): this admin console is a **static SPA
    // built by Vite**, with no server runtime of its own — the PHP kernel serves the API, and
    // the bundle talks to it over fetch. `src/app/router.tsx` uses `createBrowserRouter` with
    // **element-only routes**: grepping the tree for route `action:` / `loader:` keys returns
    // no route-config hits (the `action` matches are domain props on the creative-review
    // feature, not router config), and there is no `@react-router/dev`, no RSC entry, no
    // `createStaticHandler` / `StaticRouterProvider` import anywhere in `src/`. The advisory's
    // attack path — a server executing a route action before returning 400 — has no
    // counterpart in a client-only bundle.
    //
    // Expiry: 2026-08-31. Removed by the react-router v8 migration wave (bundled with the
    // NENE2 RR8 re-evaluation). If that wave slips past the expiry, the entry must be
    // re-argued in a PR — not silently extended.

    // GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg was here until 2026-08-04 (#203). It is not excepted any more and it
    // is not being re-argued: every chain now resolves to a patched release, so audit-ci itself
    // reports "Consider not allowlisting advisory: GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg". Deleted per this file's
    // own rule that an entry which is no longer needed goes away rather than being carried.
  ],
}
